+# F-01 — CRITICAL — `deposit()` reverts on every call against real USDT
+
+**The vault is a brick. It cannot accept a single deposit on mainnet.**
+
+## Mechanism
+
+`src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:92`:
+
+```solidity
+usdt.approve(address(aavePool), amount);
+```
+
+`usdt` is typed `IERC20`, whose `approve` is declared `returns (bool)`. Solidity therefore
+emits a `returndatasize` check after the call and reverts if fewer than 32 bytes come back.
+
+USDT at `0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7` predates ERC-20 finalisation. Its
+`approve` has **no return value**. The call succeeds, returns zero bytes, and the decode
+check reverts the whole transaction.
+
+Note the contract uses `SafeERC20` for the transfer one line earlier
+(`usdt.safeTransferFrom`, which tolerates this) but then calls `approve` *raw*. The one
+place the safety wrapper was skipped is the one place it was essential.
+
+## Evidence
+
+Confirming directly against mainnet that `approve` returns nothing:
+
+```
+$ cast call 0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7 "approve(address,uint256)" \
+    0x87870Bca3F3fD6335C3F4ce8392D69350B4fA4E2 1000 \
+    --from 0x28C6c06298d514Db089934071355E5743bf21d60 \
+    --rpc-url https://eth.drpc.org
+0x
+```
+
+`0x` — zero bytes of return data.
+
+Now the vault itself, deployed on a mainnet fork with the exact production addresses:
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path test/ForkMainnet.t.sol --match-test test_F01_DepositAlwaysRevertsOnRealUsdt -vvvv
+
+    ├─ [64468] UsdtYieldVault::deposit(1000000000 [1e9])
+    │   ├─ [35224] 0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7::transferFrom(alice: [0x328809Bc894f92807417D2dAD6b7C998c1aFdac6], UsdtYieldVault: [0x5615dEB798BB3E4dFa0139dFa1b3D433Cc23b72f], 1000000000 [1e9])
+    │   │   ├─ emit Transfer(from: alice: [0x328809Bc894f92807417D2dAD6b7C998c1aFdac6], to: UsdtYieldVault: [0x5615dEB798BB3E4dFa0139dFa1b3D433Cc23b72f], value: 1000000000 [1e9])
+    │   │   └─ ← [Stop]
+    │   ├─ [24953] 0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7::approve(0x87870Bca3F3fD6335C3F4ce8392D69350B4fA4E2, 1000000000 [1e9])
+    │   │   ├─ emit Approval(owner: UsdtYieldVault: [0x5615dEB798BB3E4dFa0139dFa1b3D433Cc23b72f], spender: 0x87870Bca3F3fD6335C3F4ce8392D69350B4fA4E2, value: 1000000000 [1e9])
+    │   │   └─ ← [Stop]
+    │   └─ ← [Revert] EvmError: Revert
+```
+
+Read the last three lines: `approve` succeeds and returns `[Stop]` (no data), and `deposit`
+immediately reverts. The USDT transfer *did* happen inside the frame, so it is rolled back
+with everything else — no funds are lost, but no deposit can ever be made.
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path test/ForkMainnet.t.sol -vv
+Ran 4 tests for test/ForkMainnet.t.sol:ForkMainnetTest
+[PASS] test_F01_DepositAlwaysRevertsOnRealUsdt() (gas: 1095813)
+[PASS] test_F01_RealUsdtApproveReturnsNoData() (gas: 35442)
+```
+
+## Fix
+
+Use `SafeERC20`'s `forceApprove`, which handles both the missing return value and USDT's
+non-zero-to-non-zero approve restriction:
+
+```solidity
+usdt.forceApprove(address(aavePool), amount);
+```
+
+`SafeERC20` is already imported and `using SafeERC20 for IERC20` is already in place — this
+is a one-word change. Better still, approve `aavePool` for `type(uint256).max` once in the
+constructor and drop the per-deposit approval entirely, saving gas on every deposit.
+
+**Add a regression test that runs against forked mainnet USDT.** No local mock will ever
+catch this class of bug; this is exactly why the 39 green tests missed it.
+
