+# F-04 — MEDIUM — The deposit fee is a wealth transfer to incumbent shareholders, and collects nothing from a sole depositor
+
+## Mechanism
+
+`deposit()` mints shares on the **net** amount but books the **gross** amount:
+
+```solidity
+shares = convertToShares(netAmount);   // net
+...
+totalAssetsStored += amount;           // gross
+```
+
+The fee is therefore distributed pro-rata across *all* shareholders — including the
+depositor who just paid it. Two consequences:
+
+1. A **sole depositor gets the entire fee back instantly**. The fee raises the value of the
+   only shares in existence, which are theirs. The vault collects nothing.
+2. With an existing pool, the fee is a **transfer from the new depositor to whoever already
+   holds shares**. A whale holding 90% of shares pockets 90% of every subsequent depositor's
+   fee for doing nothing — and can deliberately front-run a known large deposit to capture it.
+
+The NatSpec does say fees "accrue to the remaining shareholders", so (2) is arguably
+intended. But (1) means the fee is not a fee at all in the bootstrap case, and the
+front-running angle does not appear to have been considered.
+
+## Evidence
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path test/AuditAccounting.t.sol --match-test F04 -vv
+
+[PASS] testFuzz_F04_DepositFeeCollectsNothingFromALoneRoundTrip(uint256,uint256) (runs: 256, μ: 217467, ~: 217610)
+[PASS] test_F04_DepositFeeIsCapturedByIncumbentShareholders() (gas: 286529)
+Logs:
+  bob paid in                : 100000000000
+  bob's claim immediately    : 95433789954
+  alice gained for doing zero: 4566210045
+
+[PASS] test_F04_DepositFeeIsRefundedToASoleDepositor() (gas: 220476)
+Logs:
+  deposit fee bps            : 500
+  alice deposited            : 1000000000
+  alice balanceOfUnderlying  : 1000000000
+```
+
+A 5% deposit fee is charged to Alice and 100% of it is credited straight back to her
+(`1000000000` in, `1000000000` claimable). When Bob later deposits 100,000 into a pool
+Alice already dominates, Bob is instantly down **4,566.21** and Alice is up by exactly that
+amount for doing nothing.
+
+The fuzz test confirms across 256 random (amount, fee) pairs that a lone round-tripper's
+total loss never exceeds the 30bps withdrawal fee, regardless of the deposit fee set —
+i.e. the deposit fee is provably a no-op for them.
+
+## Fix
+
+Decide what the fee is actually *for* and implement that explicitly:
+
+- **To fund the protocol:** mint the fee's worth of shares to a `feeRecipient`, or keep a
+  separate `accruedFees` counter excluded from `totalAssets()`, with an owner `collectFees()`.
+- **To genuinely benefit existing holders:** keep the current maths but document that it is
+  zero-effect for the first depositor, and set the fee to 0 at launch until there is a pool
+  for it to accrue to.
+
+Whichever you choose, add `minSharesOut` to `deposit()` (see F-05/F-06) so a depositor can
+refuse a price they did not agree to.
+
+---
+
+# F-05 — HIGH — The owner can freeze all depositor funds indefinitely
+
+## Mechanism
+
+`withdraw()` carries `whenNotPaused`. There is no timelock, no maximum pause duration, no
+emergency-exit path that bypasses the pause, and ownership is a plain single-key
+`Ownable`. A compromised or malicious owner key — or simply a lost one, after `pause()` —
+locks every depositor's funds forever.
+
+## Evidence
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path test/AuditAccounting.t.sol --match-test test_F05 -vv
+
+[PASS] test_F05_OwnerCanPauseWithdrawalsAndHoldFundsHostage() (gas: 208551)
+Logs:
+  alice locked USDT after 10 years: 1000000000000
+```
+
+Alice's 1,000,000 USDT is still hers on paper and unreachable in practice, 10 years later.
+Both withdrawal attempts revert with `Pausable.EnforcedPause`.
+
+## Fix
+
+- Remove `whenNotPaused` from `withdraw()`. Pausing deposits protects users; pausing
+  withdrawals traps them. Exit should always be available.
+- If an emergency withdrawal halt is genuinely required, bound it: record `pausedAt` and
+  have the pause auto-expire for withdrawals after a fixed window.
+- Put ownership behind a multisig and a timelock before mainnet. A single EOA controlling a
+  pause switch over real user deposits is not an acceptable production posture.
+
+---
+
+# F-06 — HIGH — `setDepositFee` is unbounded, takes effect instantly, and can front-run a deposit
+
+## Mechanism
+
+```solidity
+function setDepositFee(uint256 newFeeBps) external onlyOwner {
+    depositFeeBps = newFeeBps;   // no bound, no timelock
+}
+```
+
+There is no upper bound. There is no delay. And `deposit()` has **no `minSharesOut`
+parameter**, so a depositor has no way to bound what they accept.
+
+- The owner can set a 99% fee and drain the next depositor.
+- Above 10,000 bps, `amount - fee` underflows and **all deposits revert permanently** until
+  the owner fixes it — a one-transaction self-inflicted DoS.
+- The owner can raise the fee in the block *between* a user's approval and their deposit.
+
+## Evidence
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path test/AuditAccounting.t.sol --match-test F06 -vv
+
+[PASS] test_F06_FeeAbove100PercentBricksDeposits() (gas: 45687)
+[PASS] test_F06_FeeChangeFrontRunsAnInFlightDeposit() (gas: 279630)
+Logs:
+  shares bob quoted : 1000000000000000000000
+  shares bob got    : 100000000000000000000
+
+[PASS] test_F06_OwnerCanSetA99PercentDepositFee() (gas: 283255)
+Logs:
+  deposit fee bps : 9900
+  bob paid in     : 1000000000
+  bob's claim     : 10009899
+```
+
+Bob quoted `1e21` shares off-chain, the owner's `setDepositFee(9000)` landed first, and Bob
+received `1e20` — **one tenth of his quote**, with no way to reject it. At 99%, Bob pays in
+1,000 USDT and holds a claim on **10.01 USDT**.
+
+## Fix
+
+```solidity
+uint256 public constant MAX_DEPOSIT_FEE_BPS = 100; // 1%, or whatever you can justify
+
+function setDepositFee(uint256 newFeeBps) external onlyOwner {
+    if (newFeeBps > MAX_DEPOSIT_FEE_BPS) revert FeeTooHigh();
+    ...
+}
+```
+
+Additionally:
+- Add `function deposit(uint256 amount, uint256 minSharesOut)` and revert if
+  `shares < minSharesOut`. This is the standard defence and it neutralises the front-run
+  regardless of what the owner does.
+- Route `setDepositFee` through a timelock so depositors can see a fee change coming.
+
+---
+
+# F-07 — MEDIUM — USDT can enable a transfer fee at any time, which breaks the deposit flow
+
+## Mechanism
+
+Tether's contract contains a `basisPointsRate` fee that its owner can switch on unilaterally.
+It is `0` today, but it is live code on a contract the vault has no control over.
+
+`deposit()` pulls `amount` and then supplies `amount`:
+
+```solidity
+usdt.safeTransferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);
+aavePool.supply(address(usdt), amount, address(this), AAVE_REFERRAL_CODE);
+```
+
+If USDT takes a cut, the vault receives less than `amount` but still tries to supply
+`amount`. Aave's own `transferFrom` then reverts, bricking deposits again.
+
+This does not change today's verdict — F-01 already blocks all deposits — but **it will bite
+any fix for F-01 that keeps the current flow**, so fix it at the same time.
+
+## Evidence
+
+Confirming the fee is off today and that Tether's owner can turn it on, on a mainnet fork:
+
+```
+$ cast call 0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7 "basisPointsRate()(uint256)" --rpc-url https://eth.drpc.org
+0
+$ cast call 0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7 "owner()(address)" --rpc-url https://eth.drpc.org
+0xC6CDE7C39eB2f0F0095F41570af89eFC2C1Ea828
+```
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path test/ForkMainnet.t.sol --match-test test_F07 -vv
+
+[PASS] test_F07_UsdtTransferFeeCanBeEnabledAndBreaksTheDepositFlow() (gas: 280163)
+Logs:
+  pulled  : 1000000000
+  received: 999000000
+  shortfall: 1000000
+```
+
+After the real Tether owner calls `setParams(10, 49)`, pulling 1,000 USDT delivers 999 USDT.
+A vault that pulled 1,000 and then supplied 1,000 would hold only 999 and revert.
+
+## Fix
+
+Measure the balance actually received rather than trusting the requested amount:
+
+```solidity
+uint256 balanceBefore = usdt.balanceOf(address(this));
+usdt.safeTransferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);
+uint256 received = usdt.balanceOf(address(this)) - balanceBefore;
+// use `received` for the fee split, the share maths and the Aave supply
+```
+
+---
+
+## What I checked that holds up
+
+Reporting these so you know where the review stopped, not just where it found things:
+
+- **Reentrancy.** `nonReentrant` is on both `deposit()` and `withdraw()`, and `withdraw()`
+  updates all state before its external call. No reentrancy path found.
+- **Donation / first-depositor inflation attack.** `convertToShares` reads
+  `totalAssetsStored`, an internal counter, not a token balance — so the classic share-price
+  inflation attack does **not** apply to the code as it stands. Note this protection is a
+  side effect of the F-02 bug; **if you fix F-02 by reading `aUsdt.balanceOf()`, you must add
+  inflation-attack protection at the same time.**
+- **Vault solvency.** Across a 128,000-call invariant campaign, the vault never books more
+  than it holds, and every depositor could always redeem what `balanceOfUnderlying()`
+  advertised. The failure mode is stranding value, not failing to pay:
+  ```
+  [PASS] invariant_A_NeverBooksMoreThanItHolds() (runs: 256, calls: 128000, reverts: 0)
+  [PASS] invariant_C_AdvertisedBalanceIsActuallyRedeemable() (runs: 256, calls: 128000, reverts: 0)
+  ```
+- **Arithmetic.** Solidity 0.8 checked maths plus `Math.mulDiv` throughout; no overflow or
+  division-by-zero path surfaced in fuzzing. Rounding is consistently in the vault's favour.
+- **Access control.** `onlyOwner` is correctly applied to the three privileged functions.
+  The concern is the *scope* of owner power (F-05, F-06), not missing modifiers.
+
+## What I did not cover
+
+- No formal verification; findings are test-driven.
+- Aave-side risks (reserve freeze, pause, isolation-mode caps, supply-cap rejection,
+  insufficient liquidity at withdrawal) are out of scope but worth a look — `withdraw()`
+  ignores Aave's return value, so if Aave ever returns less than requested the shares are
+  burned anyway.
+- No gas or upgrade-path review.
+
+## Summary
+
+| # | Severity | Finding |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| F-01 | **Critical** | `deposit()` reverts on every call against real USDT — the vault is a brick |
+| F-02 | **Critical** | Aave interest is never credited; a 1-year holder loses money |
+| F-03 | High | Withdrawal fee is deleted from accounting; value stranded permanently and grows |
+| F-05 | High | Owner can freeze all withdrawals indefinitely |
+| F-06 | High | `setDepositFee` unbounded, instant, and front-runs deposits |
+| F-04 | Medium | Deposit fee collects nothing from a sole depositor; transfers wealth to incumbents |
+| F-07 | Medium | USDT can enable a transfer fee, breaking the deposit flow |
+
+**Recommendation: do not deploy.** F-01 alone means the contract cannot function at all on
+mainnet. F-02 means that once F-01 is fixed, the product does not do the one thing its name
+promises. Both need fixing, and the fixes interact (see the inflation-attack note under
+F-02), so the share-accounting layer should be reworked as a unit — most cheaply by
+inheriting OpenZeppelin's `ERC4626` instead of hand-rolling it — and then re-reviewed.
+
+Before the next sign-off, I would want the test suite to include **at least one forked-mainnet
+test that exercises a real deposit and withdrawal against the real USDT and the real Aave
+pool**. That single test would have caught the two critical findings on day one.
